1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,000
An order on a set says which elements must come before which. The symbol a ⪯ b means a comes before b, or a is the same element as b.

2
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:16,000
Draw each element as a dot and each comparison as a line going upward. Two dots with no line either way are simply not compared.

3
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:23,000
Divisibility is an order. On the divisors of 12, the number 1 is below everything and 12 is above everything, while 2 and 3 sit side by side.

4
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:30,000
That is why the word is partial: the order is completely known, but not every pair has to be in a line.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:38,000
Promise one, reflexivity: every element comes before itself. In the matrix of the relation, the whole diagonal is ones.

6
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:45,000
Promise two, antisymmetry: if a comes before b and b comes before a, then a and b are the same element. No pair of ones mirrors across the diagonal.

7
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:52,000
Promise three, transitivity: if a comes before b and b comes before c, then a comes before c, whether or not the pair is written down.

8
00:00:52,000 --> 00:01:00,000
A relation with all three promises is a partial order, and the set together with it is a poset. Take any promise away and it is not one.

9
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:08,000
Two elements are comparable when one comes before the other, and incomparable when neither does. Incomparable pairs are drawn side by side.

10
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:15,000
If every pair is comparable the order is total, and the whole set is called a chain. A chain is drawn as one straight line.

11
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:23,000
A set of elements that are pairwise incomparable is an antichain. In the divisors of 12 the set containing 2 and 3 is one.

12
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:30,000
In the divisors of 12, twelve pairs are comparable and three are not, so the order is partial and not total.

13
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:38,000
Most of an order is redundant. The element b covers a when a is strictly below b and nothing sits strictly between them.

14
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:45,000
On the divisors of 12 the relation has eighteen pairs, and only seven of them are covers.

15
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:53,000
Close the seven covers transitively and the eighteen pairs come back exactly. Nothing has been lost.

16
00:01:53,000 --> 00:02:00,000
So a question that says "draw the poset" always means the covers. A line between two elements is a cover, never a long comparison.

17
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:08,000
A Hasse diagram puts a dot for each element, a line for each cover, larger elements higher, and no arrowheads and no self-loops.

18
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:16,000
Place the minimal elements on the bottom row, then each element one row above the highest element it covers.

19
00:02:16,000 --> 00:02:23,000
Read the order by walking upward: if you can get from a to b going up along the lines, then a comes before b.

20
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:30,000
Height on the page is not the order. Only a path of lines is, so a diagram can be redrawn with dots moved and the order is unchanged.

21
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:38,000
An element is minimal when nothing is strictly below it, and maximal when nothing is strictly above it. Both are local statements.

22
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:46,000
An element is the least when it is below every element, and the greatest when it is above every element. Both are global, and each is unique if it exists.

23
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:53,000
Here a and b are both below c and below d. Both c and d are maximal, and there is no greatest element at all.

24
00:02:53,000 --> 00:03:00,000
In the 2024 paper poset, 1 and 3 are minimal and 2 and 4 are maximal, so there is neither a least nor a greatest element.

25
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:08,000
Fix a subset S. An upper bound of S is above every element of S, and a lower bound is below every element of S.

26
00:03:08,000 --> 00:03:16,000
In the divisors of 12 take S as the pair 4 and 6. The only upper bound is 12, so 12 is the least upper bound.

27
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:23,000
The lower bounds are 1 and 2, and the greatest of those is 2. So the meet of 4 and 6 is 2, which is their greatest common divisor.

28
00:03:23,000 --> 00:03:30,000
Notice that 12 is not in S, and that S has no greatest element. A bound and an extreme element are different objects.

29
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:38,000
A lattice is a poset in which every pair of elements has both a least upper bound and a greatest lower bound.

30
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:46,000
Two upper bounds that cannot be compared give no join. Two lower bounds that cannot be compared give no meet.

31
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:53,000
The diamond is a lattice: 0 below three pairwise incomparable elements, all three below 1. The join of any two middles is 1 and the meet is 0.

32
00:03:53,000 --> 00:04:00,000
The 2024 poset fails the test: the pair 1 and 3 has upper bound 2 but no lower bound at all, so it has no meet.

33
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:08,000
A lattice is distributive when meet distributes over join: a ∧ (b ∨ c) equals (a ∧ b) ∨ (a ∧ c) for every triple.

34
00:04:08,000 --> 00:04:16,000
M3 fails. With a, b, c the three middles, the left side is a ∧ 1 = a and the right side is 0 ∨ 0 = 0.

35
00:04:16,000 --> 00:04:23,000
A complement of a is an element b with a ∨ b = 1 and a ∧ b = 0. In M3 each middle element has two complements.

36
00:04:23,000 --> 00:04:30,000
The power set of a three-element set is distributive and every one of its eight elements has exactly one complement, the set difference.

37
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:37,000
One: test the three promises separately. Antisymmetry is the one usually missing, and it is the one that makes the object an order.

38
00:04:37,000 --> 00:04:44,000
Two: draw the covers, not the closure. Then read comparisons by walking upward along the lines.

39
00:04:44,000 --> 00:04:52,000
Three: keep maximal apart from greatest and minimal apart from least. Count them before answering.

40
00:04:52,000 --> 00:05:00,000
Four: a lattice needs a join and a meet for every pair. Then test distributivity with one triple, and count complements.

